course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 7
title: Calling Mum and Granny; counting one to eight
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you shout for someone in Ukrainian, the name changes its ending: MAma becomes MAmo!, TAto becomes TAtu!, BAbusya becomes BAbusyu! — it is a special «calling form».
explain: So Sashko yells «MAmo!» across the flat when his juice spills, just as you'd yell «Mu-um!».
explain: Counting starts like a game of hide-and-seek: odYn, dva, try, chotyRY, p'yat, shIst, sim, vIsim.
explain: Some numbers are tricksters for English ears: try is said «trih», sim sounds like «seem», and sim (7) and vIsim (8) are easy to mix up when said fast.
fact: MAmo! is the calling form of MAma: what you shout to call Mum.
fact: TAtu! is the calling form of TAto: what you shout to call Dad.
fact: BAbusyu! is the calling form of BAbusya: what you shout to call Granny; this special form is called the vocative.
fact: maLYuk and malEcha mean little one or the little ones; adults use them about small children, and kids of nine or ten don't like being called maLYuk.
fact: odYn means one; when counting off before a race people say raz instead: raz, dva, try!
fact: dva means two; before some words, such as DIvchyny (girls), it becomes dvi.
fact: try means three; it is written like English «try» but said «trih».
fact: chotyRY means four, with the stress at the end: cho-tih-RIH.
fact: p'yat means five, and daI p'yat! means give me five, a high five.
fact: shIst means six, and it is easy to mix up with sim when said fast.
fact: sim means seven and sounds like the English word «seem».
fact: vIsim means eight; sim and vIsim sound close, seven and eight.
exercise: What do you shout to call Mum? | MAmo! | MAma! | MAmy! | MAmoyu!
exercise: What do you shout to call Dad? | TAtu! | TAto! | TAta! | TAtom!
exercise: Which is the calling form of BAbusya? | BAbusyu! | BAbusyo! | BAbusye! | BAbusya!
exercise: What is the special calling form of a name called? | The vocative | The plural | The past tense | The pet form
exercise: Who is a maLYuk? | A little one, a small child | A naughty teenager | A grandad | A cat
exercise: Why should you not call Sashko maLYuk? | Kids his age don't like being called little | It is a swear word | It means girl | It means old man
exercise: What does odYn mean? | One | Eleven | Hundred | Zero
exercise: How do kids count off before a race? | raz, dva, try! | odYn, dva, try! | try, dva, odYn! | sim, vIsim, DEvyat!
exercise: What does dva mean? | Two | Twelve | Twenty | Door
exercise: How do you say try (three)? | «trih» | Like English «try» | «tree» with a long e | «tray»
exercise: Where is the stress in chotyRY (four)? | At the end, RIH | At the start, CHO | In the middle, TIH | It has no stress
exercise: What does p'yat mean? | Five | Fifteen | Fifty | Four
exercise: What does daI p'yat! mean? | Give me five, a high five | Give me five sweets | Wait five minutes | You are five
exercise: What does shIst mean? | Six | Seven | Sixteen | Sixty
exercise: What does sim mean? | Seven | Eight | Six | Seventeen
exercise: What does vIsim mean? | Eight | Seven | Eighteen | Nine
test: Two kids shout for their mum, one «MAma!» and the other «MAmo!». Which is the proper calling form? | MAmo!, the calling form | MAma!, the only form there is | Neither, you must shout MAmy! | Neither, you must shout TAtu!
test: «try means try, like have a go» Which is right? | No, try (said «trih») means three | Yes, it means have a go, like in rugby | Yes, it means attempt | No, it means tree
test: «sim, vIsim, whatever, they're the same number» What is right? | No: sim is seven, vIsim is eight | Yes, both mean seven | Yes, both mean eight | No: sim is six, vIsim is seven
test: Sashko holds up his hand and says «daI p'yat!». What do you do? | Slap his hand, a high five | Give him five sweets from your bag | Count to five | Wait five minutes
test: «oi maLYuk, come here» a teenager says to Sashko. How will Sashko feel? | Probably annoyed — kids his age don't like it | Proud, it means champion | Hungry, it means snack time | Nothing, it's his surname
test: How many: dva cats and try dogs? | p'yat | chotyRY | shIst | sim
test: There are sim kids and one goes home. How many are left? | shIst | vIsim | p'yat | sim
test: What would happen if you shouted «BAbusya!» across the garden? | She'd understand, but BAbusyu! is the proper calling form | She would think you meant grandad | It would be rude | Nobody would understand anything
test: In a counting rhyme a cartoon says «odYn, dva, try, chotyRY, shIst». What is missing? | p'yat | sim | vIsim | dva
test: «dva girls, that's right innit» says your mate. What is right? | Before girls it is dvi: dvi DIvchyny | dva is always the same, for boys and girls | It is dvo girls | It is try girls
test: «chOtyry, stress at the start, obviously» Which is right? | No, chotyRY: stress at the end | Yes, stress at the start | Stress in the middle, TIH | There is no stress
exam: Identify the form of the noun MAma that is employed in calling the person so designated. | MAmo! | MAma! | MAmy! | MAmoyu!
exam: State the numeral that is signified by vIsim. | Eight | Seven | Six | Eighteen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of maLYuk. | It denotes a small child and is resented by children of nine or ten years | It is a respectful address to an elder | It denotes a grandparent | It is a term of praise for a victorious footballer
exam: Ascertain the expression customarily employed when counting off the commencement of a race. | raz, dva, try! | odYn, dva, try! | sim, vIsim, DEvyat! | try, dva, odYn!
